				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-460051/dichotomy#post-1424023</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sun, 22 Apr 2012 04:38:03 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>EventHorizon</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>301814</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>This sent a shiver down my spine.</p> <p>I couldn't help but imagine how it would feel to be in this situation, loading a magazine, chambering a round, flipping the safety&#8230;</p> <p>I found my breath catching as I kept reading, driven by morbid curiousity.</p> <p>Very well written, captured the emotions and thoughts of the doctor very well.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-460051#post-1415418</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-460051/dichotomy#post-1415418</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Thu, 12 Apr 2012 19:38:19 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Aburr</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1299372</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>This was excellent. Very nice prose, you build the tension evenly throughout, and the interaction with the assistant researcher fills in every other gap as far as the good doctor's character goes. Obsessive without being passionate, so broken down by the work that it's poured into his guts and turned the horrifying into a tedium. The abruptness of it-how the &quot;experiment&quot; starts, how it's ultimately terminated and how quickly he recovers from it-really sell this. A slice of life story of a life that's become a boring nightmare. I like it.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
